# Introduction

This assignment focuses on exploring various statistical methods, including hypothesis testing, confidence interval estimation, and model fitting using resampling techniques such as bootstrap and permutation tests. The primary aim is to apply these methods to both parametric and non-parametric scenarios to deepen our understanding of their practical applications in statistical analysis.

In the first task, we address a two-sample hypothesis problem where we test the null hypothesis. The two given samples will be analyzed using various statistical techniques, including the t-test and Wilcoxon rank-sum test. Additionally, we will implement bootstrap methods to estimate the significance of the differences in means between the two samples. This task will also involve comparing different sampling strategies, such as resampling with and without centering, to evaluate their effect on the analysis, and comparing these results to the outcomes from traditional hypothesis testing methods available in R.

In the second task, we simulate a linear regression model with multiple predictors, examining the impact of a predictor, x3, on the dependent variable y. Using a sample of size 200, we will apply both residual bootstrap and pairs bootstrap methods to estimate confidence intervals for the regression coefficients. A key focus will be to assess whether x3 can be excluded from the model, and we will compare the results of the two bootstrap approaches to understand how they affect model inference.

The final task involves summarizing the methodology of bootstrapping, its advantages, and its limitations based on the results from the previous exercises. We will discuss how bootstrapping can be used for constructing parametric and non-parametric confidence intervals, test statistics, and model estimates, providing a comprehensive view of its practical applications in statistical analysis. Ultimately, this assignment aims to deepen our understanding of how resampling techniques can enhance statistical inference, offering flexibility and robustness in cases where traditional assumptions may not hold.

