### 1.2 Consider different sampling schemes, such as: sampling with replacement from each group and centering both samples and then resample from the combined samples x1 and x2, for n1 and n2 times. Argue for choice what is more natural and which advantages or disadvantages may apply.

In this task, we explore different sampling schemes to assess differences between two independent samples, x1 and x2. The two primary methods considered are: (1) sampling with replacement independently within each group, and (2) centering the combined data, then resampling for n1 and n2 observations. Each approach has its own advantages and limitations. Independent sampling preserves the group structure, reflecting individual group variability, while pooled sampling focuses on directly testing the null hypothesis by assuming a symmetric distribution under H0. We will compare these schemes to determine which is more natural or appropriate in the given context.

``` {r 1.2}
bootstrap_independent <- function(x1, x2, B = 1000) {
  n1 <- length(x1)
  n2 <- length(x2)
  resampled_means <- replicate(B, {
    mean1 <- mean(sample(x1, size = n1, replace = TRUE))
    mean2 <- mean(sample(x2, size = n2, replace = TRUE))
    mean1 - mean2
  })
  resampled_means
}

set.seed(12427550)
independent_bootstrap <- bootstrap_independent(x1, x2)
summary(independent_bootstrap)

bootstrap_pooled <- function(x1, x2, B = 1000) {
  combined <- c(x1, x2)
  centered <- combined - mean(combined)
  n1 <- length(x1)
  n2 <- length(x2)
  resampled_means <- replicate(B, {
    resample1 <- sample(centered, size = n1, replace = TRUE)
    resample2 <- sample(centered, size = n2, replace = TRUE)
    mean(resample1) - mean(resample2)
  })
  resampled_means
}

set.seed(12427550)
pooled_bootstrap <- bootstrap_pooled(x1, x2)
summary(pooled_bootstrap)

```

The results from the independent bootstrap indicate that the difference in means between x1 and x2 varies within a range from a minimum of -1.11344 to a maximum of 1.38100. The negative minimum value suggests that in some bootstrap resamples, the mean of x2 was much larger than that of x1, whereas the positive maximum indicates that, in other resamples, x1 had a much larger mean than x2. The median of -0.07774 and the mean of -0.06464 suggest that the average difference in means is slightly negative, implying that x2 is generally larger than x1 in terms of the mean. The interquartile range (from -0.34803 to 0.20292) shows a moderate spread of the differences, indicating that while the two samples have differences in means, they are not excessively large. This summary provides an overview of the potential distribution of the difference in means between the two samples based on the independent resampling process.

In contrast, the pooled bootstrap resamples from a combined dataset, assuming the two samples come from the same population. This method's results have a similar range, with a minimum of -1.254975 and a maximum of 1.220136. The mean (-0.003231) and median (0.002351) are close to zero, indicating that under the pooled bootstrap approach, the difference in means is almost balanced, suggesting no strong evidence that one sample is consistently larger than the other. The interquartile range is also similar, implying comparable variability in both resampling methods.

