### 1.3 Bootstrap using both strategies mentioned above using the t-test statistic. Calculate the bootstrap p-value based on 10000 bootstrap samples and 0.95 as well as 0.99 confidence intervals. Make your decision at the significance level 0.05 or 0.01, respectively.

In this task, we utilize bootstrap resampling to test a hypothesis where:

The null hypothesis is:

\[
H_0: \mu_1 = \mu_2
\]

The alternative hypothesis is:

\[
H_1: \mu_1 \neq \mu_2
\]

Two bootstrap strategies are employed: 
(1) sampling with replacement independently from each group, and 
(2) centering the combined data before resampling. 

By generating 10,000 bootstrap samples, we calculate the bootstrap p-value and construct 95% and 99% confidence intervals for the difference in means. These results help us determine whether to reject the null hypothesis at the significance levels of 0.05 and 0.01, providing insights into the statistical significance of the observed difference.

``` {r 1.3}
bootstrap_test <- function(x1, x2, B = 10000, alpha = 0.05, method = "independent") {
  if (!is.numeric(x1) || !is.numeric(x2)) stop("Both x1 and x2 must be numeric vectors.")
  if (B <= 0 || !is.numeric(B)) stop("Number of bootstrap samples (B) must be a positive integer.")
  
  n1 <- length(x1)
  n2 <- length(x2)
  observed_stat <- tryCatch(t.test(x1, x2)$statistic,
                            error = function(e) stop("Error in calculating observed t-statistic:", e))
  
  bootstrap_stats <- numeric(B)
  
  if (method == "independent") {
    for (i in 1:B) {
      sample_x1 <- sample(x1, size = n1, replace = TRUE)
      sample_x2 <- sample(x2, size = n2, replace = TRUE)
      bootstrap_stats[i] <- tryCatch(t.test(sample_x1, sample_x2)$statistic, 
                                     error = function(e) NA)
    }
  } else if (method == "pooled") {
    combined <- c(x1, x2)
    centered <- combined - mean(combined)
    for (i in 1:B) {
      resample_x1 <- sample(centered, size = n1, replace = TRUE)
      resample_x2 <- sample(centered, size = n2, replace = TRUE)
      bootstrap_stats[i] <- tryCatch(t.test(resample_x1, resample_x2)$statistic, 
                                     error = function(e) NA)
    }
  } else {
    stop("Invalid method. Use 'independent' or 'pooled'.")
  }
  
  p_value <- mean(abs(bootstrap_stats) >= abs(observed_stat), na.rm = TRUE)
  ci <- quantile(bootstrap_stats, probs = c(alpha / 2, 1 - alpha / 2), na.rm = TRUE)
  
  list(p_value = p_value, ci = ci, observed_stat = observed_stat)
}

independent_result <- bootstrap_test(x1, x2, method = "independent")
pooled_result <- bootstrap_test(x1, x2, method = "pooled")

independent_result
pooled_result
```

The results from the bootstrap test for both the independent and pooled methods indicate that there is no significant difference between the means of x1 and x2. For both methods, the p-values are very high: 0.906 for the independent method and 0.9088 for the pooled method. These p-values are much larger than the common significance level of 0.05, suggesting that we fail to reject the null hypothesis of no difference between the means of the two samples. The observed t-statistic, which measures the difference between the sample means relative to the variability, is -0.1188053 for both methods, further indicating a negligible difference between the means. Additionally, the 95% confidence intervals for the difference in means are wide and include zero: for the independent method, the interval is from -2.502249 to 1.621847, and for the pooled method, the interval is from -1.978641 to 1.975821. This overlap with zero further suggests that the observed differences in means are not statistically significant. Overall, the bootstrap results support the conclusion that there is no evidence of a meaningful difference between the two samples based on either the independent or pooled approach.

