### 1.4 What would be a permutation version of the test? Implement the corresponding permutation test and obtain p-value and confidence intervals as in 3. to get a corresponding test decision at the same significance levels.

In this task, we implement a permutation test to evaluate the hypothesis 

\[
H_0: \mu_1 = \mu_2
\]

\[
H_1: \mu_1 \neq \mu_2
\]

A permutation test involves repeatedly shuffling the combined data between the two groups while preserving the group sizes, recalculating the test statistic (e.g., the difference in means) for each shuffle. This approach constructs a null distribution for the test statistic based on the assumption that the group labels are exchangeable under H0. By comparing the observed statistic to this null distribution, we calculate a p-value and derive confidence intervals, enabling us to make a decision at significance levels of 0.05 and 0.01. This non-parametric method is especially valuable for its flexibility and minimal reliance on distributional assumptions.

``` {r 1.4}
permutation_test <- function(x1, x2, B = 10000, alpha = 0.05) {
  if (!is.numeric(x1) || !is.numeric(x2)) stop("Both x1 and x2 must be numeric vectors.")
  if (B <= 0 || !is.numeric(B)) stop("Number of permutations (B) must be a positive integer.")
  
  combined <- c(x1, x2)
  n1 <- length(x1)
  observed_stat <- tryCatch(t.test(x1, x2)$statistic,
                            error = function(e) stop("Error in calculating observed t-statistic:", e))
  
  perm_stats <- numeric(B)
  for (i in 1:B) {
    permuted <- sample(combined)
    perm_x1 <- permuted[1:n1]
    perm_x2 <- permuted[(n1 + 1):length(permuted)]
    perm_stats[i] <- tryCatch(t.test(perm_x1, perm_x2)$statistic, error = function(e) NA)
  }
  
  p_value <- mean(abs(perm_stats) >= abs(observed_stat), na.rm = TRUE)
  ci <- quantile(perm_stats, probs = c(alpha / 2, 1 - alpha / 2), na.rm = TRUE)
  
  list(p_value = p_value, ci = ci, observed_stat = observed_stat)
}

permutation_result <- permutation_test(x1, x2)

permutation_result
```

The results from the permutation test confirm the findings from the previous tests (bootstrap and t-test), indicating no significant difference between the means of x1 and x2.

The p-value from the permutation test is 0.9099, which is far greater than the typical significance level of 0.05. This suggests that we fail to reject the null hypothesis, meaning there is no significant difference between the means of the two samples. The observed t-statistic is -0.1188053, which again indicates a very small difference between the sample means, further supporting the conclusion of no significant effect.

The 95% confidence interval for the permutation test is from -1.979562 to 1.907942. Since this interval includes zero, it reinforces the idea that the true difference in means could very well be zero, further suggesting no meaningful difference between x1 and x2.

In summary, the permutation test results, which involve resampling without any assumptions about the underlying distribution, provide further evidence that the observed difference between the two samples is not statistically significant. All methods (independent bootstrap, pooled bootstrap, and permutation test) consistently point to the conclusion that there is no significant difference between the means of x1 and x2.

