### 1.5 The Wilxocon rank sum test statistic is the sum of ranks of the observations of sample 1 computed in the combined sample. Use bootstrapping with both strategies mentioned above and obtain p-value and confidence intervals as in 3. to get a corresponding test decision at the same significance levels.

In this task, we apply the Wilcoxon rank-sum test, a non-parametric method that compares the distributions of two independent samples, x1 and x2. The test statistic is calculated as the sum of ranks of the observations in x1 when both samples are combined. To evaluate the significance of this statistic, we use bootstrapping under two strategies: (1) sampling with replacement independently from each group, and (2) centering and resampling from the combined data. These approaches generate a bootstrap distribution of the test statistic, allowing us to compute a p-value and construct confidence intervals. We then make decisions at significance levels of 0.05 and 0.01, assessing whether the observed differences are statistically meaningful.

``` {r 1.5}
suppressWarnings({
    wilcoxon_test <- function(x1, x2, B = 10000, alpha = 0.05, method = "independent") {
    if (!is.numeric(x1) || !is.numeric(x2)) stop("Both x1 and x2 must be numeric vectors.")
    if (B <= 0 || !is.numeric(B)) stop("Number of bootstrap samples (B) must be a positive integer.")
    
    n1 <- length(x1)
    observed_stat <- tryCatch(wilcox.test(x1, x2)$statistic, 
                              error = function(e) stop("Error in calculating observed Wilcoxon statistic:", e))
    
    bootstrap_stats <- numeric(B)
    
    if (method == "independent") {
      for (i in 1:B) {
        sample_x1 <- sample(x1, size = n1, replace = TRUE)
        sample_x2 <- sample(x2, size = length(x2), replace = TRUE)
        bootstrap_stats[i] <- tryCatch(wilcox.test(sample_x1, sample_x2)$statistic, 
                                       error = function(e) NA)
      }
    } else if (method == "pooled") {
      combined <- c(x1, x2)
      centered <- combined - mean(combined)
      for (i in 1:B) {
        resample_x1 <- sample(centered, size = n1, replace = TRUE)
        resample_x2 <- sample(centered, size = length(x2), replace = TRUE)
        bootstrap_stats[i] <- tryCatch(wilcox.test(resample_x1, resample_x2)$statistic, 
                                       error = function(e) NA)
      }
    } else {
      stop("Invalid method. Use 'independent' or 'pooled'.")
    }
    
    p_value <- mean(bootstrap_stats >= observed_stat, na.rm = TRUE)
    ci <- quantile(bootstrap_stats, probs = c(alpha / 2, 1 - alpha / 2), na.rm = TRUE)
    
    list(p_value = p_value, ci = ci, observed_stat = observed_stat)
  }
  
  wilcoxon_independent <- wilcoxon_test(x1, x2, method = "independent")
  wilcoxon_pooled <- wilcoxon_test(x1, x2, method = "pooled")
  
  wilcoxon_independent
  wilcoxon_pooled
})
```
The results from the Wilcoxon test for both the independent and pooled methods suggest that there is no significant difference between the two samples, x1 and x2. The p-value from the independent method is 0.6762, which is much greater than the commonly used significance level of 0.05, indicating that we fail to reject the null hypothesis. This suggests that the distributions of x1 and x2 are likely the same. The observed Wilcoxon statistic is 181, and it lies within the 95% confidence interval for the bootstrap resampled test statistics, which ranges from 127 to 270. Since the observed statistic is not extreme compared to the resampled values, this further reinforces the conclusion that there is no substantial difference between the two samples. Thus, the Wilcoxon test, both in its standard and bootstrap resampled forms, indicates that the differences between x1 and x2 are not statistically significant.

