# Task 3 - Advantages and disadvantages of bootstrapping methodology

Bootstrapping is a powerful statistical technique used for constructing parametric and non-parametric confidence intervals and test statistics, enabling robust estimation of model parameters, estimators, or test statistics. It involves resampling from the observed data (with or without replacement) to generate multiple "bootstrap samples," which are then used to compute the desired statistic or test. This approach can be applied to both parametric (e.g., t-tests, regression) and non-parametric (e.g., rank-sum tests) settings.

Advantages of bootstrapping include its flexibility, as it requires fewer assumptions about the underlying distribution, making it applicable to a wide variety of problems, especially when the data distribution is unknown or complex. It also allows for the computation of confidence intervals, p-values, and model validation measures without needing closed-form analytical solutions. Additionally, bootstrapping can be used with small sample sizes where traditional methods might fail to provide reliable results.

Disadvantages, however, include its computational intensity, particularly when the number of bootstrap samples (B) is large, requiring significant processing power and time. Additionally, while bootstrapping can provide accurate estimates, it may still be susceptible to biases or incorrect conclusions if the data itself is not representative or if the bootstrap samples are not independent or identically distributed (i.i.d.). Lastly, while bootstrapping can generate accurate confidence intervals, it may not always perform well in cases where extreme data points (outliers) heavily influence the estimates.

In tasks such as hypothesis testing or confidence interval construction, bootstrapping with different sampling schemes (like independent or pooled) or methods (like residual and pairs bootstrap) allows for greater insight into the robustness of model estimates or statistical conclusions, particularly when dealing with complex models or non-normal data distributions.
